package gauge

// The transcendental functions of this package (Sin, Cos, Tan, Asin, Acos, Atan, Atan2, Sinh, Cosh,
// Tanh, Exp, Exp2, Log, Log2, Log10, Log1p, Pow, Cbrt, Hypot, Mod) are written in Tin and call
// nothing outside it. The algorithms and coefficients are those of Go's math package (BSD
// licensed), which took them from Cephes (sin, cos, tan, atan, sinh, tanh), FreeBSD's msun (exp,
// log, log1p, cbrt) and its own code (pow, hypot, mod). One file here corresponds to one or a few
// of Go's: trig.tin (sin.go, tan.go, trig_reduce.go), atan.tin (atan.go, atan2.go, asin.go),
// hyper.tin (sinh.go, tanh.go), exp.tin, log.tin (log.go, log10.go, log1p.go), pow.tin, cbrt.tin,
// hypot.tin, mod.tin, frexp.tin (frexp.go, ldexp.go, modf.go).
//
// Each expression is kept in Go's shape. Results agree with Go's bit for bit except where a
// multiply and an add are fused differently: arm64 has a fused multiply-add and both compilers
// use it, but they do not always pick the same products (Go's fuses `x*y - a` and folds a product with
// two uses into each fused operation; Tin's rounds it once). That changes the last bit of the log
// family and of Pow for about one input in a thousand, never by more than one ulp, and leaves
// the error bounds unchanged: see notes/stdlib_verified.md, and bench/ref/gauge/corpus.go and
// corpus.tin to measure it.
//
// Constants that Go derives with exact arithmetic (4/Pi, 1/Ln10, ...) are written below as the
// nearest f64 to the exact value; computing them in f64 would be one ulp off for some of them.

const invPi4 = 1.2732395447351628 // 4/Pi
const pi4 = 0.7853981633974483 // Pi/4
const pi2 = 1.5707963267948966 // Pi/2
const pi34 = 2.356194490192345 // 3*Pi/4
const pi = 3.141592653589793
const invLn2 = 1.4426950408889634 // 1/Ln2
const invLn10 = 0.4342944819032518 // 1/Ln10
const sqrt2Half = 0.7071067811865476 // Sqrt2/2
const expLn2Hi = 6.93147180369123816490e-01 // 0x3fe62e42fee00000
const expLn2Lo = 1.90821492927058770002e-10 // 0x3dea39ef35793c76
